✅ 1. UIED (UI Element Detector) Based Approach
	•	UIED is a hybrid deep learning + heuristic tool that detects UI elements (buttons, icons, texts) from screenshots.
	•	It gives you bounding boxes and clickability heuristics using layout and design clues.

Pros:
	•	Designed specifically for mobile UI.
	•	Can detect UI types (buttons, icons, text).

Cons:
	•	Doesn’t work well on custom UI themes or complex backgrounds.
	•	Requires setup with PyTorch/Mask-RCNN.

You can use:
	•	UIED GitHub

⸻

✅ 2. OmniParser (for clickability via ML)
	•	OmniParser uses a combination of:
	•	CNN + Graph Neural Networks
	•	Extracts semantic + spatial features of elements.
	•	Trained to predict element type, including clickable vs non-clickable.

Pros:
	•	More accurate than heuristic methods.
	•	Can handle complex layouts.

Cons:
	•	Requires GPU + model training/inference setup.
	•	Needs annotated data to fine-tune.

You can use:
	•	OmniParser GitHub

⸻

✅ 3. Computer Vision + Heuristics (Your Custom OpenCV Pipeline)
	•	Use OpenCV to detect shapes, colors, and text.
	•	Create heuristic rules:
	•	Rectangular shapes with centered text/icons ⇒ likely a button
	•	Buttons have padding, border, or raised/elevated look
	•	Use grayscale pixel differences for enabled/disabled state

Steps:
	•	Convert image to grayscale or HSV
	•	Use contour detection
	•	Apply heuristics like:
if aspect_ratio ~2:1 and has_border and contains_text:
    mark as clickable

Pros:
	•	Lightweight
	•	No model training needed

Cons:
	•	Prone to errors in custom or minimal UI designs

⸻

✅ 4. Use OCR (e.g., Tesseract) + Rule-Based Clickability
	•	Use OCR to detect labels like “Submit”, “OK”, etc.
	•	Combine with bounding box + color info to guess button state.

Clickability Indicators:
	•	Words like “OK”, “Next”, “Login”
	•	If it’s grayed out → non-clickable
	•	If bright, highlighted → clickable

⸻

✅ 5. Deep Learning Approach (Train Custom Model)
	•	Annotate UI screenshots with labels: “clickable”, “non-clickable”.
	•	Train a model using:
	•	CNN or Transformer + classification head
	•	Or object detection like YOLO to also get bounding boxes

Pros:
	•	Most flexible and scalable.
	•	Can learn from real-world data.

Cons:
	•	Need a lot of labeled data
	•	Model size and speed tradeoffs

⸻

✅ 6. UI Automator XML and Screenshot Together (Ground Truth + Vision)

If you want high accuracy and are just testing:
	•	Use Android’s UI Automator dump to extract XML + screenshot.
	•	Match UI element bounds in XML with image and annotate clickability.
	•	Use it to train your CV model.

